= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a cost $c:X\times Y\to[0,+\infty]$ that is a <Borel measurable function>, a <transport map> is a measurable $T:X\to Y$ whose <pushforward measure> satisfies
$$
T_\#\mu=\nu,\qquad \mu(T^{-1}(B))=\nu(B)\quad\text{for every Borel }B\subseteq Y.
$$
The <Monge optimal transport problem> moves every source point to one destination:
$$
\boxed{\inf_{T_\#\mu=\nu}\mathbb M(T),\qquad \mathbb M(T)=\int_X c(x,T(x))\,d\mu(x).}
$$
The <Kantorovich optimal transport problem> permits mass to split. Its admissible <transport plans> are the <probability measures> on $X\times Y$ with prescribed <marginal distributions>:
$$
\Pi(\mu,\nu)=\{\pi:(p_X)_\#\pi=\mu,\ (p_Y)_\#\pi=\nu\},\qquad
\boxed{\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\mathbb K(\pi),\quad \mathbb K(\pi)=\int_{X\times Y}c\,d\pi.}
$$
Thus a <transport plan> is a <coupling of probability distributions>. The set $\Pi(\mu,\nu)$ is never empty: it contains the <product measure> $\mu\otimes\nu$. Signed costs can also be used when their integrals are well defined, for example with an integrable lower bound of the form $a(x)+b(y)$.

On the <Polish space> $\mathbb R$, take the <Dirac measures>
$$
\boxed{\mu=\delta_0,\qquad \nu=\tfrac12\delta_{-1}+\tfrac12\delta_1.}
$$
Every measurable map satisfies $T_\#\delta_0=\delta_{T(0)}$, which cannot equal $\nu$. A <transport map> cannot split an <atom of a measure>, whereas the <transport plan> $\tfrac12\delta_{(0,-1)}+\tfrac12\delta_{(0,1)}$ can.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Given any admissible <transport map> $T$, form its graph <transport plan>
$$
\pi_T=(\operatorname{Id},T)_\#\mu.
$$
For <Borel sets> $A\subseteq X$ and $B\subseteq Y$, the definition of a <pushforward measure> gives
$$
\pi_T(A\times Y)=\mu(A),\qquad
\pi_T(X\times B)=\mu(T^{-1}(B))=\nu(B).
$$
Hence $\pi_T\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)$. Integration against a <pushforward measure> also gives
$$
\mathbb K(\pi_T)=\int_X c(x,T(x))\,d\mu(x)=\mathbb M(T).
$$
The <Kantorovich optimal transport problem> therefore has at least all the competitors of the <Monge optimal transport problem>, with exactly the same costs. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\inf_{T_\#\mu=\nu}\mathbb M(T)\ \geq\ \inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\mathbb K(\pi).}
$$
If there is no admissible <transport map>, the left side is $+\infty$ by the convention $\inf\varnothing=+\infty$, so the conclusion still holds. No existence of an optimizer is needed.

